{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROUND 1: BLOCK, two findings that are the same point, ACCEPTED and verified.\n\nTHE FINDING: my AC2 asserted that tests/unit/test_plan_review_workflow_ops_seam.py 'currently loops for path in (_WORKFLOW_OPS, _LLM_CONFIG) against ONE shared _HARD_CAP', and told this ticket to split it. That is FALSE as of now. Verified by reading the file: b300's implementation already built the per-file _LOC_BOUNDS table — ((_WORKFLOW_OPS, _HARD_CAP), (_LLM_CONFIG, _HEADROOM_TARGET)) — with a comment explaining exactly why the shared constant would have gone red on this ticket's file.\n\nTHE INTERESTING PART IS HOW IT WENT STALE. I wrote that hazard into this ticket PRE-EMPTIVELY, before b300 was implemented, precisely so whoever landed first would split the loop. b300 then landed first and did it. So my own foresight became my own stale claim: the AC described the world as it was at authoring time, and a SIBLING falsified it by doing the right thing.\n\nTHE GENERALISABLE LESSON, worth more than the fix: a plan that NARRATES TRANSIENT STATE is falsified by its own siblings. 'X currently does Y, so change it' is perishable in a way that 'X must end up doing Z' is not. Rewritten as an END STATE — the per-file table already exists, so the remaining work here is exactly one constant, (_WORKFLOW_OPS, _HARD_CAP) becomes (_WORKFLOW_OPS, _HEADROOM_TARGET) — with an explicit 'do NOT re-split anything' so nobody re-does b300's work.\n\nSWEPT THE CLASS BEFORE RESUBMITTING rather than fixing only the cited criterion. Searched the whole description for perishable phrasing (currently / today / at present / as it stands / still sits). Two hits: the one just rewritten, and 'nothing currently enforces here' about the attribute-access pin — which is accurate and stays, since decide_ops.py does not exist yet so nothing can enforce anything on it. No other transient-state claims.\n\nThis is the second time on this epic that pre-emptive foresight created a stale claim (the first was b300's AC2, which asserted config.py 'has no such guard today' and was about to be falsified by d8ef). Both cases: write the required END STATE, not the current one."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785578094382012001,"uuid":"82636b4e-6830-4478-a9e9-92fbc143ad16"}